  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 239 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAfter a brief reprieve, an active and warm stretch of weather is in \nstore for Tuesday and Wednesday as the midlevel ridge leans \neastward. Moisture will begin to feed back into the area, as seen by \nan IVT tongue of 400-700 kg m-1 s-1 from the Gulf into an amplifying \ntrough across Saskatchewan and Manitoba. For the first half of the \nday Tuesday, this will lead to increased heat across the \nsouthwestern half of the CWA. Fortunately, this heat will feel less \noppressive than the recent heatwave due to dewpoints in the mid 60s, \nwhich will keep any chances of 100 degree heat indices localized to \nparts of far southeast low and northeast Missouri. That being said, \nit will still be hot with heat indices of 95 degrees or more \npossible (30-70%) Tuesday afternoon along and south of a Cedar \nRapids to Aledo line. Wednesday will also be warm, though there is \nuncertainty in high temperatures due to the potential for storms \nTuesday afternoon and evening into the overnight hours.\n\nIn addition to the potential for seasonally hot temperatures, storm \nchances will return Tuesday afternoon into Wednesday. As mentioned \npreviously, an amplifying shortwave and surface cyclone will move \nacross south-central Canada. Forcing along a cold front will support \nthe potential for showers and storms into the overnight hours. While \nthere will be sufficient shear and instability to support severe \npotential, the question of timing and capping potential introduce \nsome uncertainty. At this time, SPC has introduced a Marginal, level \n1 of 5, risk of severe storms along and north of an Ottumwa to \nMuscatine to Galena line.\n\nPast Wednesday, flow aloft transitions to more zonal, returning us \nto more seasonable temperatures for yet another brief reprieve. \nHowever, increasing heat and humidity appear possible towards the \nend of the week, continuing above normal temperatures through mid-\nSeptember.",
